---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../../schema';

const meta = {
    title: 'The Press Release SEO Checklist',
    description:
        'Fifteen checks to run on a converted press release before you publish: title tags, meta descriptions, headings, structured data, canonical tags, and AI visibility.',
    path: '/blog/press-release-seo-checklist',
    date: '2026-04-01',
};

const checks = [
    { area: 'Title tag', check: 'Under 60 characters, leads with the specific change, includes the primary topic' },
    { area: 'Meta description', check: '120–155 characters, written as a reason to click, not a summary' },
    { area: 'Headings', check: 'Exactly one H1; H2s describe sections rather than label them (use “Allocation breakdown”, not “Details”)' },
    { area: 'Canonical', check: 'A <link rel="canonical"> pointing at the live URL, so distribution copies do not compete' },
    { area: 'URL', check: 'Short, hyphenated, and readable — /press-release-to-blog-post, not /?p=1234' },
    { area: 'Figures', check: 'Every number matches the original release; units and currency symbols included' },
    { area: 'Attribution', check: 'Full name and title on first mention of every quoted speaker' },
    { area: 'Dates', check: 'Publication and event dates are explicit and machine-readable with <time datetime>' },
    { area: 'Images', check: 'At least one relevant image with descriptive alt text' },
    { area: 'Internal links', check: 'Two to four links to related pages that a reader would genuinely want next' },
    { area: 'External links', check: 'Links to authoritative primary sources for any claim the release does not cover' },
    { area: 'Structured data', check: 'Article JSON-LD with headline, datePublished, author, and publisher' },
    { area: 'FAQ markup', check: 'FAQPage JSON-LD for any reader questions the post answers' },
    { area: 'robots meta', check: 'index, follow on the published page; no accidental noindex from a staging build' },
    { area: 'Sitemap', check: 'The URL appears in sitemap.xml so crawlers discover it without waiting for links' },
];
---

<GuideLayout
    eyebrow="Checklist"
    heading={meta.title}
    intro={meta.description}
    breadcrumb={[
        { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
        { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog' },
        { name: meta.title, path: meta.path },
    ]}
    schema={[articleSchema({ headline: meta.title, description: meta.description, path: meta.path, datePublished: meta.date })]}
>
    <p>
        A converted press release usually gets the writing right and the technical layer wrong. These fifteen checks
        catch the problems that keep an otherwise good post out of search results and AI answers. Run them between
        generating the draft and hitting publish.
    </p>

    <h2>The fifteen checks</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Area</th><th>What to verify</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {checks.map(row => (
                <tr><td><strong>{row.area}</strong></td><td>{row.check}</td></tr>
            ))}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>1. Title and description</h2>
    <p>
        Release headlines are optimised for wire services, and they are usually too long and too generic for search.
        Rewrite so the title includes the specific change and the primary topic, and keep it under 60 characters so it
        is not truncated. The meta description is not a summary — it is the pitch that decides whether someone clicks.
    </p>
    <pre><code>Weak:   Acme Analytics Announces Series A Funding Round
Strong: Acme Analytics Raises $8M to Expand Forecasting Software</code></pre>

    <h2>2. Headings and structure</h2>
    <p>
        One H1 only. Every additional H1 dilutes the page topic. Section headings should carry meaning: somebody
        scanning only your headings should still understand the announcement. “Details” and “More information” tell a
        reader nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Canonical and URL</h2>
    <p>
        If you syndicate the post to a partner site or republish it elsewhere, set a canonical tag on your own version
        so the copies do not compete. Without a canonical, search engines have to guess which URL is the original,
        and they often guess wrong. Keep the URL short and readable — it is visible in every search result.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Accuracy of figures and attribution</h2>
    <p>
        This is the check most specific to press release content, and the one with real consequences. Compare every
        number against the original release. Then confirm that each speaker is named in full with their title on first
        mention. A quote without a title reads as invented.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Structured data</h2>
    <p>
        Add <code>Article</code> JSON-LD with the headline, publication date, author, and publisher. If the post
        answers reader questions, add <code>FAQPage</code> markup for those. Structured data is also what makes a page
        legible to AI answer engines: without it, models have to infer the brand, the date, and the topic from prose.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Acme Analytics Raises $8M",
  "datePublished": "2026-03-12",
  "author":    { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Acme Analytics" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Acme Analytics" }
}`}</code></pre>

    <h2>6. Internal and external links</h2>
    <p>
        Internal links help readers continue and help crawlers understand which pages matter. Link to pages a reader
        would genuinely want next, not to everything. External links to primary sources — the issuing body, the
        standard, the dataset — build credibility and give AI systems a source to verify against.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Indexability</h2>
    <p>
        Confirm the published page is not inheriting a <code>noindex</code> from a staging build, and confirm the URL
        appears in <code>sitemap.xml</code>. Both are silent failures: nothing in the interface tells you the page is
        invisible.
    </p>

    <h2>8. AI visibility</h2>
    <p>
        AI answer engines summarise rather than rank. To be quotable, state the facts plainly near the top, keep
        figures in a consistent format, define the organisation and the date explicitly, and avoid burying the core
        claim under scene-setting. A post that answers “what changed, for whom, and by how much” in the first
        paragraph is far likelier to be cited.
    </p>

    <h2>Quick pre-publish pass</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Title under 60 characters with the specific change.</li>
        <li>Meta description 120–155 characters.</li>
        <li>One H1, descriptive H2s.</li>
        <li>Canonical set to the live URL.</li>
        <li>Every figure and name verified against the release.</li>
        <li>Article and FAQ JSON-LD present.</li>
        <li>URL in the sitemap, page indexable.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        To produce the draft these checks apply to, use <a href="/">the PressFolio converter</a>. For the conversion
        workflow itself, see <a href="/blog/how-to-convert-a-press-release-into-a-blog-post">the step-by-step guide</a>.
    </p>
</GuideLayout>